WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.694
Hey, good morning, Ziad.

00:00:01.694 --> 00:00:03.106
Hope you slept okay.

00:00:03.106 --> 00:00:05.719
It's Tuesday, November twenty-fourth.

00:00:05.719 --> 00:00:10.731
A dry, mild one, with a regular workday and something on there tonight.

00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:13.626
Here's one to think about while you move.

00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:19.274
What do you multiply a lease's money factor by to turn it into an interest rate?

00:00:19.274 --> 00:00:20.685
Answer's at the end.

00:00:20.685 --> 00:00:22.168
Weather's easy today.

00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:31.911
Dry all day and mild, probably into the sixties this afternoon, and the wind picks up a bit late, with gusts near twenty on the ride home.

00:00:31.911 --> 00:00:33.887
Thursday's the one to watch.

00:00:33.887 --> 00:00:41.865
The storm now looks like rain from late morning into early Friday, one to two inches, with gusts in the thirties.

00:00:41.865 --> 00:00:42.854
Okay, the day.

00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:47.019
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:47.019 --> 00:00:48.996
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:48.996 --> 00:00:51.537
And plans on there at eight tonight.

00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:55.844
So there's a nice gap after work to eat something real first.

00:00:55.844 --> 00:00:56.832
Alright, news.

00:00:56.832 --> 00:00:58.597
The Nvidia story flipped.

00:00:58.597 --> 00:01:03.539
Last week the board said it wouldn't bring Jensen Huang back as C.E.O.

00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:06.222
On Monday it did, in a unanimous vote.

00:01:06.222 --> 00:01:10.034
The board said it heard from senior people in the U.S.

00:01:10.034 --> 00:01:13.352
government and found no national security risk.

00:01:13.352 --> 00:01:18.224
A foreign intelligence investigation into him is still going, though.

00:01:18.224 --> 00:01:24.366
The finance chief, who'd been running things in the meantime, goes back to her old job.

00:01:24.366 --> 00:01:24.931
The U.N.

00:01:24.931 --> 00:01:28.531
climate summit in Turkey ended with no deal at all.

00:01:28.531 --> 00:01:35.309
More than a hundred and ninety countries couldn't agree on what to say about coal, oil, and gas.

00:01:35.309 --> 00:01:35.873
The E.U.

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:43.780
and the countries most at risk refused a version that left fossil fuels out, after pushback led by Saudi Arabia.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:50.346
It's the first time since two thousand nine that those talks ended without a final agreement.

00:01:50.346 --> 00:01:52.323
And a strange one from math.

00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:56.065
Anthropic put out a long paper saying one of its A.I.

00:01:56.065 --> 00:02:03.548
models proved a famous problem from nineteen seventy-nine, about how hard certain calculations really are.

00:02:03.548 --> 00:02:06.513
A computer proof checker signed off on it.

00:02:06.513 --> 00:02:12.161
Apparently OpenAI posted its own proof of the same thing about five hours later.

00:02:12.161 --> 00:02:18.586
No human mathematicians have checked either one yet, so it's a claim for now, not a result.

00:02:18.586 --> 00:02:21.127
Okay, a small one for your calendar.

00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:28.681
There's a winter market from the Bushwick Vendors Market crew on George Street, Saturday, December twelfth.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:32.070
No hours posted yet, so call it a hold-the-date.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:39.695
But it's walking distance, and it's a good excuse to find a gift that didn't come in a box from a warehouse.

00:02:39.695 --> 00:02:42.166
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:42.166 --> 00:02:46.190
Pick one small decision you've been circling for a while.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.767
Which thing to buy, when to call someone back, whether to say yes to something.

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:56.215
Give yourself until the end of the ride home, then just decide.

00:02:56.215 --> 00:03:01.581
Most of the weight of a small decision is carrying it around, not making it.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:04.193
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:04.193 --> 00:03:08.429
Remember printing parts for that iMac turned into a monitor?

00:03:08.429 --> 00:03:11.041
Here's what actually separates P.L.A.

00:03:11.041 --> 00:03:14.006
and P.E.T.G., and why one of them strings.

00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:15.983
Start with glass transition.

00:03:15.983 --> 00:03:19.442
Plastic doesn't go straight from solid to liquid.

00:03:19.442 --> 00:03:28.620
Before it melts, there's a temperature where the long chains inside it loosen up, and the part goes from rigid to kind of rubbery.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.597
That's the glass transition.

00:03:30.597 --> 00:03:31.303
For P.L.A.

00:03:31.303 --> 00:03:34.480
it's low, around fifty-five to sixty Celsius.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.044
P.E.T.G.

00:03:35.044 --> 00:03:37.092
holds out until about eighty.

00:03:37.092 --> 00:03:43.940
So for anything sitting near warm electronics, like a screen with a backlight behind it, P.E.T.G.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.140
is the safer bet.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:45.564
P.L.A.

00:03:45.564 --> 00:03:48.952
can slowly sag there, even without ever melting.

00:03:48.952 --> 00:03:50.435
Next, layer adhesion.

00:03:50.435 --> 00:03:56.789
A printed part is a stack of thin layers, and each new one has to weld onto the one below.

00:03:56.789 --> 00:04:03.496
That weld happens when hot chains from the new layer wiggle into the old layer before it cools.

00:04:03.496 --> 00:04:04.061
P.E.T.G.

00:04:04.061 --> 00:04:08.014
prints hotter and stays soft longer, so it fuses better.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:08.438
P.L.A.

00:04:08.438 --> 00:04:10.485
is stiffer, but more brittle.

00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:14.721
Either way, the joins between layers are the weak direction.

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:21.357
So you want to orient a part so the load runs along the layers, not trying to peel them apart.

00:04:21.357 --> 00:04:22.628
Now the stringing.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:25.734
That same soft, sticky quality is the catch.

00:04:25.734 --> 00:04:28.841
When the nozzle jumps across a gap, P.E.T.G.

00:04:28.841 --> 00:04:32.936
keeps oozing and pulls out thin hairs, like melted cheese.

00:04:32.936 --> 00:04:41.690
The fixes are pulling the filament back a little before each jump, printing a few degrees cooler, and keeping the spool dry.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:42.255
P.E.T.G.

00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:49.103
soaks up moisture from the air, and that water boils in the nozzle, which makes the oozing worse.

00:04:49.103 --> 00:04:50.373
So, short version.

00:04:50.373 --> 00:04:50.797
P.L.A.

00:04:50.797 --> 00:04:53.409
is easy and stiff, but it hates heat.

00:04:53.409 --> 00:04:53.974
P.E.T.G.

00:04:53.974 --> 00:04:57.857
is tougher and handles heat, but it's messier to print.

00:04:57.857 --> 00:05:02.587
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, plans at eight.

00:05:02.587 --> 00:05:05.905
Make that one decision by the time you're home.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.164
The answer: twenty-four hundred.

00:05:08.164 --> 00:05:10.000
Have a good Tuesday, Ziad.
